      "output": "Title: Solved: A jug holds two litres of cola. If \nyou pour out 500ml, how much \nis left? [Math]\nURL: https://www.gauthmath.com/solution/1792785022383110/A-jug-holds-two-litres-of-cola-If-you-pour-out-500ml-how-much-is-left-\nPublished: 2024-03-06T13:50:30.000Z\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\n# A jug holds two litres of cola. If you pour out 500ml, how much is left?\n...\n### Answer\n\n1500 ml left.\n...\n1. Subtract 500 ml from 2000 ml: 2000 - 500 = 1500 ml.\n2. Convert 2 litres to ml: 2 litres = 2000 ml.\n\n---\n\nTitle: Geetha brought 2 l and 250 ml of water in a bottle. Her friend drank 300 ml from it. How much of water is remaining in the bottle? | Shaalaa.com\nURL: https://www.shaalaa.com/question-bank-solutions/geetha-brought-2-l-and-250-ml-of-water-in-a-bottle-her-friend-drank-300-ml-from-it-how-much-of-water-is-remaining-in-the-bottle_194307\nPublished: 2021-02-24T23:26:39.000Z\nAuthor: Shaalaa.com\nHighlights:\nGeetha brought 2 l and 250 ml of water in a bottle. Her friend drank 300 ml from it. How much of water is remaining in the bottle?\n...\n#### SolutionShow\n...\nQuantity of water Geetha brought = 2 l 250 ml\n...\n= 2 × 1000 + 250 ml\n...\n= 2000 + 250 ml\n...\n= 2250 ml\n...\nQuantity of water her friend drank = 300 ml\n...\nRemaining water = 2250 – 300\n...\n= 1950 ml\n...\n= 1 litre 950 ml\n...\nRemaining water = 1 litre 950 ml.\n\n---\n\nTitle: 7 Geetha brought 2 / and 250 m / of water in\nURL: https://studyx.ai/homework/108596301-7-geetha-brought-2-and-250-m-of-water-in-a-bottle-her-friend-drank-300-m-from-it-how-much\nPublished: 2024-10-25T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nProblem 7: Geetha brought 2 liters and 250 milliliters of water in a bottle. Her friend drank 300 milliliters from it. How much water is remaining in the bottle?\n...\nFirst, convert 2 liters to milliliters: 2 liters= 2× 1000 milliliters= 2000 milliliters\n...\nAdd the 250 milliliters to the 2000 milliliters: 2000 milliliters+ 250 milliliters= 2250 milliliters\n...\nSubtract the 300 milliliters that her friend drank: 2250 milliliters− 300 milliliters= 1950 milliliters\n...\nConvert the remaining water back to liters and milliliters: 1950 milliliters= 1 liter 950 milliliters\n...\nSo, the remaining water in the bottle is 1 liter 950 milliliters​.\n\n---\n\nTitle: PSLE Mathematics Guide: Capacity & Volume Units | eduKateSG\nURL: https://edukatesg.com/psle-mathematics-learning-guide-capacity-volume-units/\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\n## Remaining capacity = full capacity − current amount\n...\nA tank holds 12 L when full and currently contains 7.35 L.\n...\nRemaining capacity = 12 − 7.35 = 4.65 L.\n...\n2 L + 750 mL.\n...\nAnswer: 2.75 L.\n\n---\n\nTitle: Class 3 Metric Units Capacity Worksheet: Litres & Millilitres\nURL: https://www.vedantu.com/worksheets/class-3-maths-metric-units-capacity-l-ml\nPublished: 2025-08-11T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\n1. A water bottle contains 1 L of water. Rohan drank 300 mL from it. How much water is left in the bottle in mL?\n...\n2. A milkman delivered 2 L of milk to Mrs. Sharma's house and 1 L 500 mL of milk to Mrs. Gupta's house. How much milk did he deliver in total? (Give your answer in L and mL).\n...\n3. A recipe for a cake\n...\nTo convert litres (L) to millilitres (mL), you multiply the number of litres by 1,000. This is because 1 Litre is equal to 1,000 Millilitres. For example, to convert 2 litres to millilitres, you calculate 2 x 1,000 = 2,000 mL.\n\n---\n\nTitle: A jug holds two litres of cola If you pour\nURL: https://studyx.ai/homework/112528543-a-jug-holds-two-litres-of-cola-if-you-pour-out-500ml-how-much-is-left-1-litre-1-5-litres\nPublished: 2024-12-05T01:57:39.000Z\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nCorrect Answer:\n...\n1,5 litres\n...\nThe jug initially holds 2 litres of cola. When 500ml (which is equivalent to 0.5 litres) is poured out, the remaining amount can be calculated by subtracting the poured out amount from the initial amount:\n...\nThus, 1.5 litres of cola remain in the jug.\n\n---\n\nTitle: [FREE] Evan has 6 liters of water in a container. He pours out 2,300 milliliters. How much water is left in the - brainly.com\nURL: https://brainly.com/question/26899744\nPublished: 2022-03-08T01:42:49.000Z\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nAfter pouring out 2,300 milliliters from a 6-liter container, Evan has 3.7 liters of water left. This was determined by converting liters to milliliters, subtracting the poured amount, and converting back to liters.\n...\nEvan has 6 liters of water in a container and pours out 2,300 milliliters. To determine how much water is left, we first need to convert all measurements to the same unit. Given that 1 liter equals 1,000 milliliters, Evan's initial 6 liters of water is equivalent to 6,000 milliliters. After pouring out 2,300 milliliters, the calculation to find the remaining volume of water is:\n...\n6,000 milliliters - 2,300 milliliters = 3,700 milliliters\n...\nTo convert back to liters for easier understanding, we divide by 1,000:\n...\n3,700 milliliters ÷ 1,000 = 3.7 liters\n...\nTherefore, after pouring out 2,300 milliliters, Evan has 3.7 liters of water left in the container.\n...\nEvan initially has 6 liters of water, which converts to 6,000 milliliters. After pouring out 2,300 milliliters, he has 3,700 milliliters left, which is equivalent to 3.7 liters. Therefore, Evan has 3.7 liters of water remaining in the container.\n...\nfind out how much water Evan has left after pouring out 2,300 milliliters, we need to follow these steps:\n...\nConvert liters to milliliters: Evan starts with 6 liters of water. Since 1 liter is equal to 1,000 milliliters, we convert liters to milliliters:\n...\nSubtract the amount poured out: Evan pours out 2,300 milliliters. We subtract this from the total amount of water he had:\n...\nConvert back to liters: Now, we convert the remaining amount back to liters for easier understanding. Since there are 1,000 milliliters in a liter, we divide by 1,000:\n...\nTherefore, after pouring out 2,300 milliliters, Evan has 3.7 liters of water left in the container.\n...\n3,700\n...\n1 liter = 6000\n...\nso subtract 6,000-2,300=3,700\n\n---\n\nTitle: Subtraction of Capacity in Liters and Milliliters | Problems, Online Practice, Tests, Worksheets, Quizzes, Assignments | Class 4 | NCERT (CBSE and ICSE) | Edugain Global\nURL: https://www.edugain.com/2-39-7266-7271-7273/math/NCERT-CBSE-and-ICSE-/Class-4/Subtraction-of-Capacity-in-Liters-and-Milliliters\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: Edugain Content Team\nHighlights:\nCapacity in Liters\n...\nMilliliters\n...\nHour Clock Time\n...\n.1.3.2. Conversion from 24-Hour Clock Time to\n...\n12-Hour Clock Time \n\n 4.1.3.3\n...\n12-Hour Clock Time\n...\n24-Hour Clock Time \n\n 4.1.4. Use of 'AM' and 'PM' \n\n 4.1.5. Addition and Subtraction of Time \n\n 4.1.5.1. Addition of Time \n\n 4.1.5.2. Subtraction of Time \n\n 4.1.5.3. Word Problems on Time \n\n 4.2. Calendar \n\n 4.2.1. Days of the Week \n\n 4.2.2. Months of the Year \n\n 4.2.3. Ordinary, Leap, and Century Years \n\n 4.2.5. Word Problems on Calendar \n\n 5. Solid Shapes (The Way The World Looks) \n\n 5.1. Identify Different Solid Shapes (Cuboid, Cube, Cone, Cylinder, Sphere, Prism, and Pyramids) \n\n 5.2. Solid Shapes Around Us \n\n 5.3. Vertices, Edges and Faces of Solid Objects \n\n 5.4. Net of a 3-D Shape \n\n 6. Multiplication (The Junk Seller) \n\n 6.1. Multiplicand, Multiplier and Product \n\n 6.2. Multiplication - Properties and Facts \n\n 6.2.1. Order Property of Multiplication \n\n 6.2.2. Grouping Property of Multiplication \n\n 6.2.3. Multiplicative Property of One \n\n 6.2.4. Multiplicative Property of Zero \n\n 6.2.5. Distributive Property of Multiplication over Addition \n\n 6.2.6. Identify the Property of Multiplication \n\n 6.3. Multiplication by Multiples of 10 \n\n 6.3.1. Multiplication by 10, 100, and 1000 \n\n 6.3.2. Multiplication by a Multiple of 10, 100, and 1000 \n\n 6.3.3. Mixed Problems on Multiplication 